  "id": "shoulder-flexion-raise",
  "name": "Front Raise",
  "definition": "A strict single-joint raise in which an open-chain upper arm flexes from the side to shoulder height in the sagittal plane while the elbow angle, neutral humeral rotation, torso, and lower body remain controlled. Scapular upward rotation and posterior tilt are normal coupled elevation actions, not fixed-ratio claims. Upper trapezius is a secondary dynamic contributor to the declared upward rotation, not merely a stabilizer: a loaded arm-elevation model validated against EMG places superior trapezius among the greatest activity and positive-work contributors during flexion, and a small unweighted held-position study observed its activity increasing gradually in both flexion and abduction. Those sources support a categorical dynamic role without claiming equal magnitude or target emphasis across paths. The initial single-arm standing setup is a deliberately disclosed mechanics-derived application of direct dumbbell-flexion evidence: the reviewed study anchors the implement, action, and shoulder-height position but does not make that exact posture and laterality an experimentally compared result.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "ackland-2008-shoulder-moment-arms",
    "holzbaur-2005-upper-extremity",
    "seth-2019-shoulder-work",
    "ludewig-2009-multiplanar-humeral-elevation",
    "morihara-2011-scapular-muscles-flexion-abduction",
    "parry-2012-dumbbell-shoulder-elevation",
    "hecker-2021-deltoid-flexion-abduction"
  ],
